<%
  project_name = manifest.fetch("project", {}).fetch("name", "This application")
  tenancy_mode = manifest.fetch("tenancy", {}).fetch("mode", "multi_account")
  selected_modules = manifest.fetch("modules", {}).select { |_name, enabled| enabled }.keys.sort
  data_risk = manifest.fetch("data_risk", {})
%># CLAUDE.md

Project guidance for AI agents working on **<%= project_name %>**.

Generated by the T40 Enterprise Application Starter v<%= starter_version %>. This file
describes the architecture actually installed — follow it in preference to generic Rails
habits. The control record is `config/t40/control-manifest.yml`
(`bin/rails t40:controls` to view).

## Tech stack

- **Framework:** Rails 8, modular monolith (no microservices, no Rails Engines)
- **Database:** PostgreSQL — also hosts Solid Queue (jobs) and Solid Cache
- **Frontend:** Hotwire (Turbo + Stimulus), importmap, vanilla CSS with the T40 design
  tokens in `app/assets/stylesheets/design-tokens.css` (OKLCH). User-facing views use
  Phlex components. Extend that component architecture; do NOT add Tailwind, shadcn,
  ViewComponent or a React layer.
- **Authentication:** passwordless magic-link, built on Rails primitives — this project
  does not use Devise
- **Authorisation:** capability predicates with default-deny `authorize!` — not Pundit
- **Testing:** RSpec + FactoryBot (`spec/`)

## Manifest selections

- **Tenancy mode:** `<%= tenancy_mode %>`
- **Selected modules:** <%= selected_modules.empty? ? "none — core baseline only" : selected_modules.join(", ") %>
- **Data risk flags:** <%= data_risk.empty? ? "not recorded" : data_risk.map { |k, v| "#{k}: #{v}" }.join(", ") %>

The manifest that produced this application is `t40-manifest.yml` at the project root.

## Authentication (passwordless)

- `Identity` is the global person (email); `User` is their membership of an `Account`
  with one role (`owner`, `admin`, `member`). One identity can belong to many accounts.
- Sign-in: `SessionsController` emails a short-lived (15 minutes), single-use
  `MagicLink` code. Responses never reveal whether an address is known
  (anti-enumeration) — preserve this property in any change to the sign-in flow.
- `Session` records are server-side: rotated on sign-in, revocable individually and
  globally (`Session.revoke_all_for(identity)`), with inactivity (2 weeks) and absolute
  (12 weeks) lifetimes.
- Never add password columns, password forms or Devise. Enterprise SSO arrives via the
  `oidc` module only.

## Tenancy rules

- `Account` is the tenant root. Every tenant-owned model MUST `include AccountScoped`
  (non-null `account_id`, `for_account` scope, no default_scope) and get a foreign key
  plus account-scoped indexes in its migration.
- `Current.account` is resolved ONLY from the authenticated identity's memberships
  (`AccountScoping` concern). NEVER resolve the account from params, headers or
  subdomains.
- Queries for tenant data go through `Model.for_account(Current.account)` or
  associations off `Current.account` — never unscoped finds on tenant models.
- Cross-account staff access uses `SupportAccessGrant.grant!` /
  `SupportAccessGrant.with_grant` (time-limited, reasoned, audited). No other route.

## Authorisation

- Default deny. Controllers, jobs and exports assert capabilities:
  `authorize!(:can_manage_users?)` or `authorize!(:can_export_data?, record)`.
  Passing a record also asserts its `account_id` matches `Current.account`.
- Capabilities are predicate methods on `User::Role` (`app/models/user/role.rb`):
  `can_manage_users?`, `can_manage_account?`, `can_export_data?`,
  `can_view_audit_log?`, `can_grant_support_access?`. Owners satisfy `admin?`.
- Add new capabilities as predicates there, record them in
  `docs/assurance/role-capability-matrix.md`, and write a negative test (member must be
  denied). Hiding UI is a convenience, never the boundary.

## Audit events

- `AuditEvent.record!(action:, target:, changes:)` — raises on failure; use INSIDE the
  business transaction for critical actions so the action cannot succeed without
  evidence.
- `AuditEvent.record(...)` — rescues and logs; non-critical breadcrumbs only.
- Actor, account, impersonator, correlation id, release and time are auto-filled from
  `Current`. `changes:` is redacted (`AuditEvent::Redactor`) — still, never pass raw
  `params`.
- Audit events are append-only: updating or destroying raises. Do not "fix" audit rows.
- Controller-level breadcrumbs use `record_activity(action, subject:, metadata: {})`
  (`ActivityEvents` concern) with allowlisted metadata keys only.

## Background jobs

- `ApplicationJob` includes `T40::JobContext`: account, identity and correlation context
  serialise into the payload and restore into `Current` around perform.
- Any job touching tenant data declares `requires_tenant!` — it then fails closed
  (raises `T40::JobContext::MissingTenantContext`, discarded not retried) without an
  account.
- Each job declares its own `retry_on` / `discard_on`. Jobs must be idempotent and safe
  to retry; failed jobs must stay visible (never rescue-and-swallow in `perform`).

## Operational surfaces

- Health: `/health/live`, `/health/ready` (DB + queue heartbeat). No auth; never leak
  config there.
- Maintenance/read-only modes: `T40_MAINTENANCE=1` / `T40_READ_ONLY=1` (see
  `docs/operations/maintenance-mode.md`).
- Feature flags: `T40::Flags.enabled?(:key)` — missing flags are false; every flag needs
  description, owner, removal_by and default in `config/t40/feature-flags.yml` (boot
  validation enforces this). Durable customer capabilities belong in
  `Account` entitlements (`account.entitled?(key)`), not flags.
- Secrets: references only — see `config/t40/required-environment.yml`. Never write
  secrets to code, logs, fixtures or this file.

## Commands

```bash
bin/setup                        # or: bundle install && bin/rails db:prepare
bin/rails server                 # http://localhost:3000
bin/rails t40:bootstrap          # first account + owner (ADMIN_EMAIL, ACCOUNT_NAME)

bundle exec rspec                # full test suite
bundle exec rspec spec/requests  # one directory
bundle exec rubocop              # lint (rubocop-rails-omakase)
bundle exec brakeman -q          # static security scan
bin/rails t40:controls           # show the control manifest
bin/rails t40:flags:report       # feature flags incl. expired
```

Development sign-in: request a code at `/session/new`; in development the magic-link
code is available via `MagicLink.last.code` in the console or the mailer preview/log.

## Documentation map

The assurance pack lives in `docs/` and is part of the deliverable — keep it current as
you change the system:

- `docs/README.md` — index and how documents map to controls
- `docs/architecture/` — context, data flows, trust boundaries, threat model, ADRs
  (add an ADR for every hard-to-reverse decision)
- `docs/privacy/` — data inventory, DPIA screening (gate before production), retention,
  subject rights, complaints
- `docs/operations/` — incident runbook, backups (restore-test gate), alerts,
  maintenance, support access, access review
- `docs/assurance/` — assurance matrix, role-capability matrix, ASVS L2 assessment,
  accessibility checklist, release evidence
- `SECURITY.md` + `public/.well-known/security.txt` — vulnerability disclosure

When you add a model with personal data: `include PrivacySubject`, implement
`privacy_export` and `privacy_erase!`, and add it to `docs/privacy/data-inventory.md`
and `docs/privacy/subject-rights.md`. Soft deletion (`SoftDeletable`) is recoverability,
not erasure.

## Non-negotiables

1. Never weaken anti-enumeration, tenant scoping, default-deny authorisation or audit
   append-only behaviour — the spec suite asserts all of them.
2. Never log or store codes, tokens, secrets or raw params (parameter filtering and the
   audit redactor exist for a reason — do not bypass them).
3. This starter does not claim certification: it supports evidence gathering; it does
   not by itself provide ISO 27001, SOC 2, Cyber Essentials, WCAG conformance or legal
   compliance.
